  "worked_solution": "To solve the inequality $f(x)\\ge 0,$ we need to find the values of $x$ for which the curve $y=f(x)$ lies above the $x$-axis or touches this axis.\n\nFirst, we factor the polynomial $f(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={x}^{3}+6{x}^{2}+9x \\\\  & =x({x}^{2}+6x+9) \\\\  & =x({x}^{2}+3x+3x+9) \\\\  & =x(x(x+3)+3(x+3)) \\\\  & =x(x+3)(x+3) \\\\  & =x(x+3)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe roots of the polynomial $f(x)={x}^{3}+6{x}^{2}+9x$ are $x=0$ and $x=-3,$ where\n\n- $x=-3$ has multiplicity $2,$ and\n\n- $x=0$ is a simple root.\n\nSo, we can graph the curve as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007649/assets/24adb4d2477b6db5b51f538f5f3f57c197cbe3d1df53ad1b4e7e9479879f18dd.png)\n\nSince we are interested in solving $f(x)\\ge 0,$ we look for the part of the curve that is above the $x$-axis or touches this axis.\n\nBased on the graph, the solution is \n\n$$\nx\\in {-3}\u22c3[0,\\infty ).\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
